 C Preprocessor

 Project Overview

The C Preprocessor is a mini-project developed using the C programming language to understand and implement the basic operations performed by a C preprocessor.

The project processes a C source file and generates an intermediate `.i` file by handling preprocessor directives such as header file inclusion, comment removal, and macro substitution.

 Features
- Comment Removal: Removes single-line (`//`) and multi-line (`/* */`) comments from the source file.
- Header File Inclusion: Processes #include directives and includes the contents of supported header files.
- File Inclusion: Reads the specified header file and copies its contents into the output file.
- Macro Processing: Stores and substitutes defined macros in the source code.
- Intermediate File Generation:Generates an output file with the `.i` extension.

Technologies Used
- Programming Language: C
- Operating System: Ubuntu Linux
- Compiler: GCC
- Editor: Linux Terminal / Text Editor

Project Structure
mypreprocessor/
│
├── main.c
├── comment_remove.c
├── header_inclusion.c
├── preprocessor.h
├── inputfile.c
├── Makefile
└── README.md


How to Compile and Run

Step 1: Navigate to the project directory
cd mypreprocessor

Step 2: Compile the project
make

Step 3: Run the executable
./mypreprocessor

Step 4: Check the generated output

The processed source code will be generated in an intermediate file with the `.i` extension.

Concepts Learned
- File handling in C
- Reading and writing files
- String manipulation
- Preprocessor directives
- Functions and modular programming
- Header file handling
- Makefile and compilation
- Working with Linux commands

Learning Outcome
Through this project, I gained practical experience in file handling, modular programming, and understanding how a C preprocessor processes source code before compilation.


